                <pb facs="0879" n="777" sameAs="frus:frus1927v03/pg777"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.672/549: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the High Commission in
                        Turkey</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1927-01-25T17:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">January 25,
                                1927—5 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>16. Your telegram No. 13, January 24, 3 p.m. Most of the leading newspapers,
                    regardless of their political views, have carried editorials criticizing the
                    action of the United States Senate on the treaty with Turkey. There has been
                    little or no editorial comment approving the Senate’s action.</p>
                <p>To overemphasize the significance of editorial comment would be unwise. Many
                    leading newspapers in the past few months in their editorials have urged the
                    approval of the treaty, but this fact apparently had no particular influence on
                    the action of the Senate.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Kellogg</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
